#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 4.6.18
# DESCRIPTION
# Given a position function s(t), find:
#    (a) The velocity and acceleration functions
#    (b) Position, velocity, speed, & acceleration at t = B
#    (c) The times when the particle is stopped
#    (d) The times when the particle is speeding up/slowing down

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Rates of change - engineering and physics)
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('4.6')
## Problem1('18')
## KEYWORDS('Functions', 'Applied Problems', 'Velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Speed', 'Position')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

#    s(t) = (t^2 - A)^2
Context("Numeric")
    ->flags->set(reduceConstants => 0, reduceConstantFunctions => 0);
Context()->variables->add(t => 'Real');

$Atmp    = random(2, 9);
$A       = $Atmp**2;
$Posfunc = Formula("t^4 - 2 $A t^2 + $A^2")->reduce;

#   (a) Velocity & Acceleration functions
$Velfunc = $Posfunc->D('t')->reduce;
$Accfunc = $Velfunc->D('t')->reduce;

#   (b) Position, velocity, speed & acceleration at specified time
do { $B = random(1, 9) } until $B < $Atmp;

$Post    = $Posfunc->eval(t => $B)->reduce;
$Velt    = $Velfunc->eval(t => $B)->reduce;
$Acct    = $Accfunc->eval(t => $B)->reduce;
$Spdt    = Compute("abs($Velt)");
$Postsub = $Posfunc->substitute(t => $B);
$Veltsub = $Velfunc->substitute(t => $B);
$Acctsub = $Accfunc->substitute(t => $B);

#   (c) Time when particle is stopped (solve 4t^3 - 4At = 0)
#          (student enters comma-separated list)
$Endpt   = Compute("$Atmp");
$Stopped = List(0, $Endpt);

#   (d) Times when particle is speeding up/slowing down
#       -  Velocity and acceleration must be same sign
if    ($Atmp == 3) { $Bound = Formula("sqrt(3)"); }
elsif ($Atmp == 6) { $Bound = Formula("2sqrt(3)"); }
elsif ($Atmp == 9) { $Bound = Formula("3sqrt(3)"); }
else               { $Bound = Formula("$Atmp/sqrt(3)"); }

$Speedup  = Interval("(0,$Bound) U ($Atmp,inf)");
$Slowdown = Interval("($Bound,$Atmp)");

BEGIN_PGML
The function [` s(t) `] describes the position of a particle moving along a coordinate line,
where [` s `] is in feet and [` t `] is in seconds.
[``` s(t) = [$Posfunc], \qquad t \ge 0 ```]
If appropriate, enter answers in radical form. Use *inf* to represent [`\infty`].

a) Find the velocity and acceleration functions. 

    [# [. [`v(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Velfunc} .]*
     [. [`a(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Accfunc} .]* 
     #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

b) Find the position, velocity, speed, and acceleration at [`t = [$B]`].

    [# [. Position ([`\textrm{ft}`]):   .] [. [_]{$Post} .]*
      [.  Velocity ([`\textrm{ft/ses}`]): .] [. [_]{$Velt} .]*
      [.  Speed ([`\textrm{ft/sec}`]):   .] [. [_]{$Spdt} .]*
      [.  Acceleration ([`\textrm{ft/sec}^2`]): .] [. [_]{$Acct} .]*
    #]{center => 0, align => 'rl', padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

c) At what times is the particle stopped? Enter as a comma-separated list.

    [`t =`] [_]{$Stopped}


d) When is the particle speeding up? Slowing down? Enter using interval notation.

    [# [. Speeding Up:   .] [. [_]{$Speedup} .]*
      [.  Slowing Down: .] [. [_]{$Slowdown} .]*
    #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

a) To find the velocity function, take the derivative of the position function, [`s(t) `]. 

    [``` s'(t) = v(t) = [$Velfunc]. ```]

    To find the acceleration function, take the derivative of the velocity function.

    [``` v\;'(t) = a(t) = [$Accfunc]. ```]

    Note that this is also the second derivative of the position function.

b) To find the position, velocity, and acceleration at [`t = [$B]`], 
    evaluate each of the functions at this value.

    [``` \begin{aligned}
         s([$B]) &= [$Postsub] = [$Post] \;\mbox{ft} \\
         v([$B]) &= [$Veltsub] = [$Velt] \;\mbox{ft/sec} \\
         a([$B]) &= [$Acctsub] = [$Acct] \;\mbox{ft/sec}^2\\
       \end{aligned}
    ```]
    
    To find the speed, use the absolute value of the velocity.

    [``` speed = |[$Velt]| = [$Spdt] \;\mbox{ft/sec}. ```]

c) The particle is stopped whenever its velocity equals 0.
    So solve the equation [`v(t) = 0`].

    [``` [$Velfunc] = 0 \quad \Rightarrow t = [$Stopped]. ```]

d) To determine when the particle is speeding up or slowing down, 
    we need to look at the signs of the velocity and acceleration:

    - If the velocity is positive, then a positive acceleration 
will cause it to speed up
    - If the velocity is negative, then a negative acceleration
will cause it to speed up

    Therefore, the particle is speeding up whenever the velocity and 
    acceleration are of the same sign. So if they are both positive we have,
    
    [``` [$Velfunc] > 0 \;\mbox{AND}\; [$Accfunc] > 0 ```]
    Solving these inequalities yields 
    [` t > [$Atmp] \;\mbox{AND}\; t > [$Bound] `]. 
    In interval notation this is [` ([$Atmp],\infty) `].

    If they are both negative we have,
    [``` [$Velfunc] < 0 \;\mbox{AND}\; [$Accfunc] < 0 ```]
    Solving these inequalities yields 
    [` 0 < t < [$Atmp] \;\mbox{AND}\; t < [$Bound] `]. 
    In interval notation this is [` \left(0,[$Bound]\right) `].

    Therefore the particle is _speeding up_ over 
    [` \left(0,[$Bound]\right) \cup ([$Atmp],\infty) `].

    The particle must therefore be _slowing down_ over the interval not covered above.
    Therefore, the particle is slowing down over the interval 
    [` \left([$Bound],[$Atmp]\right). `]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
